Hey, not sure you're all aware that undefined gets encoded to NULL at DbQuery level, which means that all the code passing optional values needs to explicitly remove undefined values like here (otherwise you're risking setting some column to null when undefined was passed explicitly)

{noformat}// SomeTable.ts
export interface UpdateData {
   foo: number;
   bar?: string;
}

export class SomeTable {
   public async updateById(ctx, id: number, update: UpdateData): Promise<void> {
      await this.db.update(ctx, Tablenames.someTable, id, update);
   }
}

//SomeService.ts

export interface SomeData {
   id: number
   foo: number;
   bar?: string;
}

export class SomeService {
   public async handle(ctx, data: SomeData): Promise<void> {
     // need to hack around data.bar being undefined, or this will be nulled
     await this.table.updateById(ctx, foo.id, {foo: data.foo, ...{foo.bar ? {bar: foo.bar} : {}}){noformat}

I don't really want to start discussion about difference between {foo?: string} and {foo: string | undefined} when strict compilation is still optional

Wanted to know how much of an issue this is for you whether:

all good - this is intuitive and I make use of it every day

whatever - coding is hard and writing db queries should be too

this is weird - only null should be null and undefined should be treated as no update to the column at all

Related mysql doc: [https://github.com/mysqljs/mysql#escaping-query-values|https://github.com/mysqljs/mysql#escaping-query-values|smart-link] 
Here's a test proving this behaviour in DbQuery [https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-nodejs-common/blob/8b4046a65be1c68e0840f4c9b0b6851a3954c8dd/spec/db/DbQuery-spec.ts#L115|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-nodejs-common/blob/8b4046a65be1c68e0840f4c9b0b6851a3954c8dd/spec/db/DbQuery-spec.ts#L115]
